{
 "segments": [
  {
   "text": "Case Eleven.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 1.838,
   "pause": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "The Window Table.",
   "start": 2.4379999999999997,
   "end": 3.569,
   "pause": 1.4
  },
  {
   "text": "The Kettle and Gull has four little tables in a row, running from the door at one end to the big bay window at the other. Agnes Bell calls them the first, second, third and fourth tables, counting from the door, so the fourth table is the window table.",
   "start": 4.969,
   "end": 20.67,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "On Wednesday, Kerensa Hale came in early for a pot of tea. She took off her silver seagull brooch to polish it, set it on the windowsill beside the window table, and then hurried out without it when she saw her bus. The windowsill can only be reached from the window table itself. To reach it from anywhere else, you would have to climb over the window table.",
   "start": 21.52,
   "end": 42.384,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Four guests had lunch that day, one at each table, and none of them got up from their seats until they left. When Kerensa came rushing back at two o'clock, the brooch was gone.",
   "start": 43.234,
   "end": 53.815,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Agnes had spent the whole lunchtime in the kitchen with a tray of pasties, and young Pip Carew had done the serving. Pip couldn't remember exactly who sat where, but he did remember a few things.",
   "start": 54.665,
   "end": 66.527,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "\"Captain Quill sat at a table right next to Wenna's,\" he said. \"Loveday wasn't at either end of the row. Mr Spargo sat closer to the door than Captain Quill did. And I'm sure that Wenna wasn't at the window table, because she complained that she couldn't see the boats.\"",
   "start": 67.377,
   "end": 83.142,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Agnes wiped the flour off her hands and drew four little squares in her mind.",
   "start": 83.992,
   "end": 88.493,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Here are Pip's clues again. Captain Quill sat right next to Wenna. Loveday was not at either end. Mr Spargo sat closer to the door than Captain Quill. Wenna was not at the window table. The four guests were Mr Spargo, Loveday, Captain Quill and Wenna, one at each table.",
   "start": 89.343,
   "end": 106.815,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Can you solve it? Who sat at the window table and has the brooch?",
   "start": 107.915,
   "end": 111.435,
   "pause": 1.2
  },
  {
   "text": "Think about it.",
   "start": 112.635,
   "end": 113.659,
   "pause": 0.9
  },
  {
   "text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
   "start": 114.559,
   "end": 119.786,
   "pause": 4.5
  },
  {
   "text": "The Solution.",
   "start": 124.286,
   "end": 125.31,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Captain Quill sat at the window table, so he has the brooch.",
   "start": 126.41,
   "end": 129.717,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Loveday was not at either end, so she was at the second or third table.",
   "start": 130.567,
   "end": 134.897,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Suppose Loveday was at the third table. Wenna was not at the window table, so she was at the first or second. If Wenna was at the first table, Captain Quill had to be beside her at the second, and Mr Spargo would be left with the window table, which is further from the door than the captain. That breaks a clue. If Wenna was at the second table, the captain had to be at the first, beside her, and again Mr Spargo would be at the window, further from the door. That breaks the same clue. So Loveday was not at the third table.",
   "start": 135.747,
   "end": 167.784,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "So Loveday was at the second table. Wenna can't be at the window, and if she were at the first table, the captain would have to be at the second, which is Loveday's. So Wenna was at the third table, the captain was beside her at the window table, and Mr Spargo was at the first table, closest to the door.",
   "start": 168.634,
   "end": 186.895,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Captain Quill turned out his coat pocket and there was the seagull brooch. A gust of wind had come in through the open window, and he had pocketed the brooch to stop it from blowing into the harbour, and then he had forgotten all about it. He was very embarrassed, and paid for Kerensa's next pot of tea.",
   "start": 187.745,
   "end": 205.601,
   "pause": 0.0
  }
 ],
